From f9ef8f32245678b61cfcda58febfde049e8ee0e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raiju contributors <raiju@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:55:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: request bodies as streams: fetch with a ReadableStream
 body, req.body in Bun.serve (bodyStreamThreshold, maxRequestBodySize),
 Socket.pause and resume

Neither end of an HTTP request could stream its body. fetch took a string or bytes, and
Bun.serve held a request whole (up to 64 MiB) before calling the handler, so a server could not
pass a large body on without holding all of it.

fetch: a ReadableStream body is sent as it is read, chunked on HTTP/1.1 (as it is when the
caller gives a Content-Length) and as DATA frames on HTTP/2 through a deferred data provider
that waits for the stream's window. fetchWrite settles once a chunk is on the wire, so at most
one chunk waits in memory and a slow server slows the source; a source that fails aborts the
request, and a request that ends first (an early answer, a failure, an abort) cancels the
source. Such a request cannot be repeated: a 307 or 308 is returned instead of followed, and a
retry on a reused connection happens only while the first 1 MiB of the body is still held. A
server that answers early and closes has its answer read. ReadableStream's underlying source
gains pull(controller).

Bun.serve: a body over bodyStreamThreshold reaches the handler as soon as its head is in; the
connection's later bytes feed req.body (Content-Length, or an incremental chunked decoder with
trailers). Once 512 KiB wait unread the socket is not read until the handler takes them. A body
nobody reads is dropped when the request is answered (up to 32 MiB more, past that the
connection closes) and the connection serves its next request. A client that leaves or
half-closes fails the stream. maxRequestBodySize answers a larger declared length with 413 and
fails a chunked body past it. Expect: 100-continue is answered for a streamed body. text(),
json() and the others read the stream whole. Cancelling a body that is all in frees its stream
at once; the connection is looked up before the stream is freed.

Socket.pause() and resume() take a socket off the read interest and back (Node's pair).

Tests: tests/fetch/native_request_body.tov (36 checks) sends stream bodies to the HTTP/2 test
origins in all three modes: small, empty, sized, large and odd-sized bodies, backpressure from
a server that does not read, an early 413 that cancels the source, a failing source, a 307
returned, and the next request on the same origin. tests/http/body_stream.tov with
body_stream_client.py (19 checks, run by tests/http.rs when python3 is installed) sends big
bodies to Bun.serve over raw sockets: chunk-by-chunk reads, a small body, text(), chunked
framing with extensions and trailers (whole and byte by byte), an ignored body, an early
answer, 100-continue, both 413 cases, clients that leave or cut a body short, backpressure and
the server's peak memory, a relay into fetch, and a cancelled body that is all in.
tests/run/native_socket_pause.tov checks that a paused socket is not read and resume() reads
on.
